.\" Original maux page, MIT, 2026-09-19.
.\" Historical-source decision: House assembler and dialect; Research UNIX as(1) describes an incompatible PDP-11 assembler.
.\" Rendered by make man-pages; staged on play image in /usr/man/.
.TH NAS 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
nas \- assemble maux 6502 source
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nas
.RB [ \-o " object" ]
.I source
.SH DESCRIPTION
Assemble source in the maux Merlin-derived dialect.
The source directives select flat binary or Merlin REL output.
The default output name is A.OUT; -o must precede the source name.
For example:
.PP
.B nas \-o hello.o hello.s
.PP
REL objects can be linked with ld(1).
NAS is the assembler used by the native make + nas + ld rebuild.
It is distinct from the older Merlin/Sweet16 as(1).
.PP
The supported dialect includes 6502 instructions, enabled 65C02
instructions, labels, expressions, include directives, data directives
and ENT/EXT relocation records. It is the project's implemented subset,
not every Merlin macro facility.
Source may be in either text convention: maux text (high bit set,
lines ended by $8D, what vi(1) writes) or ProDOS-standard text (low
ASCII, lines ended by $0D); a PUT file may differ from the file that
names it. An undefined symbol is an error, as in Merlin:
.PP
.B nas: unknown label NAME (FILE line N)
.PP
FILE and N name the file being read, a PUT file counting its own
lines. An EXT symbol is not undefined.
Diagnostics identify source errors; a failed assembly must not be used
as a successful build product.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/NAS  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
as(1), ld(1), nm(1), make(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on successful assembly, 1 for reported failures.
Diagnostics (usage, unknown label, branch out of range, phase error, pass failures, write failures) go to standard error; the report "nas: N bytes -> FILE"
goes to standard output.
.SH BUGS
There is no -l listing option. NAS itself is a host-built PLASMA/native executable; the native world rebuild does not rebuild its compiler or cooker. NAS is staged on native toolchain images, not the ordinary play command roster.
